IMPORT prelude

-- House to House and Street Collections Act 1947 (Singapore) -- what counts as a collection (s 2), the licence requirement and the
-- offences of promoting or collecting without one (s 3), the grounds on which the Commissioner of Police may refuse or revoke a
-- licence and the 14-day appeal to the Minister (s 4), the badge and false-information offences (s 6), the duty to give a name and
-- address to the police (s 7), penalties and the liability of those managing a body (s 8), and the court's order over the money
-- collected (s 9(2)).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/HHSCA1947.txt. The deposit says it "incorporates all
-- amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021". Its Legislative History lists no
-- amending Act after the 1959 Transfer of Powers Ordinances and Modification of Laws Order; the later entries are revised editions
-- (1970, 1985, 2014).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0060 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a
-- sharper question yet. The Act is short (ten sections), so this row covers nearly all of it. Not encoded: the regulation-making
-- power (s 5) beyond the offence of contravening a regulation, the content of any regulations (none were retrieved), the licence
-- application procedure and conditions (s 4(1) to (3)), the Minister's handling of an appeal (s 4(8)), court jurisdiction (s 9(1)),
-- and what a Gazette exemption (s 10) may contain -- an exemption is modelled only as a flag that the person is exempt.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions of the example rows. No pipeline, no
-- coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `House to House and Street Collections Act 1947`

§§ `2 -- what is a collection`

-- s 2(1): "collection" means "an appeal to the public or any class of the public, made by means of visits from house to house or of
-- soliciting in streets or other public places, or by both such means, to give, whether for consideration or not, money or other
-- property not being money or property due or about to fall due from the donors under or by virtue of any written law, contract or
-- other legal obligation". "house" includes a place of business.
-- So selling something door to door "for consideration" can be a collection, and the purpose need not be charitable; the text names
-- no purpose at all. An appeal made by neither means (online, by post) is outside the definition.
DECLARE Appeal HAS
    `is made to the public or a class of the public` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is made by visits from house to house` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is made by soliciting in streets or public places` IS A BOOLEAN
    `asks for money or property legally due from the donor` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the appeal` IS AN Appeal
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appeal is a collection` IF
        `the appeal`'s `is made to the public or a class of the public`
    AND (`the appeal`'s `is made by visits from house to house` OR `the appeal`'s `is made by soliciting in streets or public places`)
    AND (NOT `the appeal`'s `asks for money or property legally due from the donor`)

§§ `3 and 10 -- collections must be licensed`

-- s 3(2): a person who promotes a collection, where a collection is made pursuant to the promotion, commits an offence "unless there is
-- in force, throughout the period during which the collection is made, a licence authorising him, or authorising another under whose
-- authority he acts" -- fine up to $5,000 or imprisonment up to 2 years or both.
-- s 3(3): a person who "acts as a collector" commits an offence unless there is in force "at all times when he so acts" a licence
-- authorising a promoter under whose authority he acts, or the collector himself. No special penalty: s 8(1) applies.
-- s 10: the Minister may by Gazette notification exempt any person from the Act, subject to conditions. (Modelled as a flag; that a
-- person in breach of an exemption's conditions is not exempt is an inference.)
-- The text states no mental element for either offence: a collector who believed the promoter was licensed is not excused by its words.
GIVEN `promoted a collection` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a collection was made under the promotion` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the promoter's own licence was in force throughout` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a licence of a person he acts under was in force throughout` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `exempted by the Minister` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the promoter commits an offence` IF
        `promoted a collection`
    AND `a collection was made under the promotion`
    AND (NOT `the promoter's own licence was in force throughout`)
    AND (NOT `a licence of a person he acts under was in force throughout`)
    AND (NOT `exempted by the Minister`)

GIVEN `acted as a collector` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the collector's own licence was in force at all times` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the licence of the promoter he acts under was in force at all times` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the collector was exempted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the collector commits an offence` IF
        `acted as a collector`
    AND (NOT `the collector's own licence was in force at all times`)
    AND (NOT `the licence of the promoter he acts under was in force at all times`)
    AND (NOT `the collector was exempted`)

§§ `4 -- refusal, revocation and appeal`

-- s 4(1): on an application in the prescribed manner the Commissioner of Police "shall ... grant" a licence, subject to the following
-- provisions and to conditions. s 4(4): the Commissioner "may, in his discretion, refuse to grant a licence or may at any time revoke"
-- one if it appears to him -- (a) the amount likely to be applied to the purpose is "inadequate in proportion to" the proceeds; (b)
-- excessive remuneration is likely to be, or has been, retained out of the proceeds; (c) a licence would facilitate an offence, or
-- "force, threat or compulsion" is likely to be or has been used; (d) the applicant is not fit and proper because convicted of an
-- offence involving fraud or dishonesty, or one a licence would facilitate; (e) failure of due diligence over collectors or badges;
-- (f) an unlawful society, a purpose "illegal, fictitious or objectionable on grounds of public policy", or refusal "otherwise
-- desirable in the public interest"; (g) false information, or refusal or neglect to furnish information reasonably required.
-- s 4(5): written notice stating the grounds and the right of appeal to the Minister, whose decision "shall be final".
-- s 4(6): where the refusal or revocation was based on any ground in (4)(f) the Commissioner "may decline to state his reasons".
-- s 4(7): the appeal must be brought within "14 days from the date on which notice is given".
DECLARE Grounds HAS
    `(a) too little of the proceeds would reach the purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(b) excessive remuneration out of the proceeds` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(c) an offence facilitated or force or threats used` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(d) the applicant has a dishonesty conviction` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(e) no due diligence over collectors or badges` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(f) unlawful society, improper purpose or public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
    `(g) false or withheld information` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the grounds` IS A Grounds
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Commissioner may refuse or revoke` IF
        `the grounds`'s `(a) too little of the proceeds would reach the purpose`
    OR  `the grounds`'s `(b) excessive remuneration out of the proceeds`
    OR  `the grounds`'s `(c) an offence facilitated or force or threats used`
    OR  `the grounds`'s `(d) the applicant has a dishonesty conviction`
    OR  `the grounds`'s `(e) no due diligence over collectors or badges`
    OR  `the grounds`'s `(f) unlawful society, improper purpose or public interest`
    OR  `the grounds`'s `(g) false or withheld information`

GIVEN `the grounds` IS A Grounds
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Commissioner may decline to state reasons` IF
    `the grounds`'s `(f) unlawful society, improper purpose or public interest`

-- Counting: day 0 is the date notice is given. That an appeal brought on the 14th day after is in time is an inference from "within
-- 14 days from the date"; the text does not say how the days are counted.
GIVEN `days after notice` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appeal to the Minister is in time` IF
    `days after notice` AT MOST 14

§§ `6 and 7 -- badges, false information, and the police demand`

-- s 6(1): an offence for any person who, "in connection with any appeal to the public or any class of the public for subscriptions or
-- contributions" -- (a) displays or uses a prescribed badge or certificate of authority other than one he is authorised to use; or (b)
-- displays or uses any badge, device, emblem, certificate or other document "intended, calculated or likely to cause any person to
-- believe" he is an authorised collector for a licensed collection "when such is not the case".
-- s 6(2): an offence where a person furnishing information for the Act "knowingly or recklessly makes any statement which is false in
-- any material particular".
-- s 7: a police officer may require a person he believes to be acting as a collector "to declare to him immediately" his name and
-- address; failing to comply is an offence, fine up to $100.
GIVEN `in connection with an appeal for contributions` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `used a prescribed badge or certificate he is not authorised to use` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `used something likely to make people think he is an authorised collector` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `is in fact an authorised collector for a licensed collection` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the badge offence is committed` IF
        `in connection with an appeal for contributions`
    AND (    `used a prescribed badge or certificate he is not authorised to use`
         OR  (`used something likely to make people think he is an authorised collector` AND (NOT `is in fact an authorised collector for a licensed collection`)))

GIVEN `furnished information for the Act` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the statement is false in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `made it knowingly or recklessly` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the false information offence is committed` IF
    `furnished information for the Act` AND `the statement is false in a material particular` AND `made it knowingly or recklessly`

GIVEN `a police officer believing him a collector required his name and address` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `declared them immediately` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `refusing the police demand is an offence` IF
    `a police officer believing him a collector required his name and address` AND (NOT `declared them immediately`)

§§ `8 -- penalties and the liability of managers`

-- s 3(2): promoting without a licence -- fine up to $5,000 or imprisonment up to 2 years or both.
-- s 7: refusing a name and address -- fine up to $100 (no imprisonment is mentioned).
-- s 8(1): any other offence "for which no special penalty is provided by this Act or by any of its regulations" -- fine up to $1,000
-- or imprisonment up to 6 months or both. (Regulations may prescribe their own penalties: s 5(2)(h). None were retrieved, so a
-- regulation offence is modelled only where the regulation sets none.)
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `promoting an unlicensed collection`
    `collecting for an unlicensed collection`
    `contravening a regulation that sets no penalty of its own`
    `using a badge without authority`
    `knowingly or recklessly furnishing false information`
    `refusing a name and address to the police`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine in dollars` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `promoting an unlicensed collection` THEN 5000
    WHEN `refusing a name and address to the police` THEN 100
    OTHERWISE 1000

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `promoting an unlicensed collection` THEN 24
    WHEN `refusing a name and address to the police` THEN 0
    OTHERWISE 6

-- s 8(2): where an offence is committed by "any corporation, society, association or body of persons", every person "charged with,
-- or concerned or acting in, the control or management" of its affairs "shall be deemed to be guilty of that offence" unless he proves
-- that "through no neglect or omission on his part, he was not aware" of it, or that "he took all reasonable steps to prevent its
-- commission". The burden is on the manager.
GIVEN `the body committed an offence under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `takes part in managing the body` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved he was unaware through no neglect of his` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved he took all reasonable steps to prevent it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the manager is deemed guilty` IF
        `the body committed an offence under the Act`
    AND `takes part in managing the body`
    AND (NOT `proved he was unaware through no neglect of his`)
    AND (NOT `proved he took all reasonable steps to prevent it`)

§§ `9(2) -- what happens to the money`

-- s 9(2): at the end of a trial "when the trial results in a conviction", the court "may make an order for the return to its owner,
-- if known, or for the forfeiture or confiscation" of money or property which "in the opinion of the court, was obtained by or in
-- consequence of the commission of an offence under this Act".
GIVEN `the trial ended in a conviction` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the court thinks the money was obtained by the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may order return or forfeiture` IF
    `the trial ended in a conviction` AND `the court thinks the money was obtained by the offence`
